Wolf-Rayet (WR) stars are evolved, hydrogen-depleted massive stars exhibiting strong, broad emission-line spectra driven by powerful stellar winds; they are subdivided into nitrogen-sequence (WN) and carbon-sequence (WC) classes with ionization-based subtypes (e.g., WN5–7, WC8–9). Standard single-star evolutionary scenarios produce WR stars either through intrinsic radiation- and metallicity-driven mass loss in the very massive star (VMS) regime, or through envelope removal during a luminous blue variable (LBV) phase including giant eruptions; alternatively, in binary systems, Roche-lobe overflow or other forms of binary interaction can strip the hydrogen envelope, possibly together with brief intermediate stripping phases.

Consider the observed Galactic and Local Group WR population, with Westerlund 1 (Wd1) — whose WR census is dominated by WN5–7 and WC8–9 subtypes that show infrared dust excesses and X-ray luminosities indicative of binarity — taken as a representative test case of lower-luminosity WR stars with strong winds.

Determine the evolutionary pathways that produce the bulk of this observed WR population, in particular the lower-luminosity strong-wind subtypes such as WN5–7 and WC8–9. Quantify the relative contributions of:
(i) intrinsic radiation-driven mass loss in single stars;
(ii) envelope loss through LBV-type winds and giant eruptions in single-star evolution;
(iii) binary interaction (mass transfer, common-envelope evolution, stripping by a companion);
(iv) short-lived intermediate stripping phases;
and reconcile the resulting predicted current structures, luminosities, surface compositions, and binary fractions with the observed WR populations whose configurations or formation routes are not accounted for by standard single-star evolutionary predictions.

